# Manuscript PDF page 224

                        HERALDS OF THE CEMÍ



   Not hard. One dull, weighted ring. The sound a mason makes a
thousand times a day, listening for a flaw. He tilted his head. Struck
again. Dust lifted off the top course. The boy glanced down, startled,
and Tomás said something that made him lower the level to the
ground.
   When that ring reached the Armory, the trolley wheels shivered.
   The floor under me went to scaffolding. 1913, a plank forty feet up, a
mason in a flat cap tapping a brick. And across the world, in another
century, another man doing the same thing in the same breath: tink,
tink, two men listening to a wall.
   I felt a weight come off my wrist that I’d had no right to carry.



Hispaniola was many places.
   In Haina, under the stopped chimney, Eliseo stood among twenty-
two players: the old Haitian woman with the long horn, the young
drummer who had once told her she was late. They had assembled
after our call. Wilfredo’s uncle had come in last Easter’s dress and was
passing a bottle he swore was medicinal. She blew a note and the
drums replied. The young man followed her breath and waited his
turn. He didn’t give up his part; he laid it down where hers could be
heard.
   In a courtyard above Port-au-Prince, Mama Tete’s cousin Mireille
stood among her neighbors with a radio switched off on a chair. They
had good reasons to distrust a stranger’s talk of unity. Mama Tete
hadn’t asked for forgiveness on anybody’s behalf. She had asked for an
answer.
   They answered.
   And in the old city of Santo Domingo, behind a green door, my
grandmother sat in her doorway with a basin of water between her feet
and a neighbor beside her, and dipped two fingers in. She didn’t know
what she was doing. She did it as she had done everything for seventy-
nine years: eyes on the street, in case somebody walked by.




